/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Part 1 is the singly reinforced beam at its limit: block at xu,max, steel Ast,lim, moment Mu,lim.
   - fsc is the design stress in the compression steel (Annex G-1.2), in N/mm².
   - Some textbooks write the steel force as (fsc − 0.446 fck) Asc to subtract the displaced concrete; that raises Asc a few per cent.
   - The compression steel strain is less than the 0.0035 at the top:
   - fsc is then read from the design stress-strain curve of the bar.
   - For Fe250 (mild steel, elastic then flat) fsc = 0.87 fy = 217.5 N/mm² once εsc ≥ 0.87 fy/Es = 0.00109.
   - Fe415 design curve points (strain, N/mm²): (0.00144, 288.7), (0.00192, 324.8), (0.00241, 342.8), (0.00276, 351.8), (0.00380, 360.9); interpolate between them.
   - Compression steel also reduces long-term deflection (deflection control).
*/
