/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The slab is stressed most next to the web (shear lag), so the code uses a uniform stress over an effective width bf. bw is the web breadth, Df the slab thickness and L0 the distance between points of zero moment (the span of a simply suppor
   - bf may not exceed the web plus half the clear distance to the next beam on each side.
   - If Mu ≤ Mf = 0.36 fck bf Df (d − 0.42 Df), the neutral axis lies in the flange and the beam is designed as a rectangle of width bf:
   - For an analysis, compare T = 0.87 fy Ast with 0.36 fck bf Df: if T is smaller, xu = T/(0.36 fck bf) lies in the flange.
   - Most floor T-beams fall in this case; minimum steel is 0.85 bw d/fy.
   - If xu > Df, split the compression into the web (width bw, usual block) and the overhangs (width bf − bw).
   - yf = Df when Df ≤ 3/7 xu, otherwise yf = 0.15 xu + 0.65 Df ≤ Df.
   - With xu = xu,max this gives the limiting moment of the T-beam, Mu,lim,T.
*/
